// expect: 10
// A STAGE THAT IS A BARE OP REFERENCE IS APPLIED, never minted: `x |> put`
// performs `put(x)` in the frame the pipe stands in, as a pipe into a
// function calls it and a literal stage binds its parameter there. The judgment charged the
// stage nothing, which is right — and the lowering built the op's partial
// record and called it, so under `!Alloc` the allocation audit refused the
// executable with an internal invariant (measured 2026-10-13 on boot
// 53213e36: `a closure record allocates in pipe_it, whose row Put does not
// say Alloc`). The same program with a call in place of the pipe ran to 10.
effect Put {
  put(v: Int) -> Int
}

handler doubler {
  put(v) => resume(v * 2),
}

fn pipe_it(x) with !Alloc = x |> put

fn main() = pipe_it(5) ~> doubler
